Spring : Spring is a Java framework that helps developers build applications easily.
Spring -->
1. Creates objects
2. Connects objects
3. Manages object lifecycle
4. Reduces code
5. Makes applications easier to maintain
--> Spring contains a container called IoC Container
    IoC = Inversion of Control
    --> The container is responsible for:
        1. Reading configurations
        2. Creating objects
        3. Storing objects
        4. Injecting dependencies
        5. Managing object lifecycle

here, Configuration means telling Spring how your application should be set up and 
      how different objects should be created and connected.
Types of Configuration in Spring : 
1. XML-Based Configuration
2. Java-Based Configuration
3. Annotation-Based Configuration(Most commonly used today.)
Common Configuration Annotations :
| Annotation       | Purpose                    |
| ---------------- | -------------------------- |
| `@Configuration` | Marks configuration class  |
| `@Bean`          | Creates bean manually      |
| `@Component`     | Creates bean automatically |
| `@Service`       | Service layer bean         |
| `@Repository`    | Database layer bean        |
| `@Controller`    | MVC controller bean        |
| `@Autowired`     | Inject dependency          |

Code Example :
Student.java -->

package com.example;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class Student 
{
    public void study() 
    {
        System.out.println("Studying Spring");
    }
}

Main.java -->

package com.example;

import org.springframework.context.ApplicationContext;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.AnnotationConfigApplicationContext;

public class Main 
{
    public static void main(String[] args) 
    {
        ApplicationContext context = new AnnotationConfigApplicationContext("com.example");
        Student student = context.getBean(Student.class);
        student.study();
    }
}

Explanation :
in Student class -->

package com.example; tells Java which package the class belongs to.

import org.springframework.stereotype.Component; imports Component annotation.

@Component Tells Spring to create and manage this class as an object(Student).

in Main class -->

//ApplicationContext context = new AnnotationConfigApplicationContext("com.example");
ApplicationContext context is a Spring container that creates, Stores and manages objects.
new AnnotationConfigApplicationContext("com.example") Starts Spring container, Scans package and Finds classes
having annotations like @Component, @Bean etc etc.

Student student=context.getBean(Student.class); Ask Spring to Give me Student object.
context = Spring container
getBean() = fetch bean
Student.class = bean type to fetch

student.study(); Calls study method.


